 4 and 5,\n\nGriggs and Ho [12], Theorem 2.1 and Etienne [10]), characterized all T-cyclic\n\npartitions for arbitrary N. This result is given as follows.\n\nTheorem.[10] Let N= 1 + 2 + · · · +k+r,0≤r≤k. Then a partition λ of N\n\nis T-cyclic if and only if λ has the form\n\n(k+δ k, k −1 + δ k−1,...,1 + δ 1, δ 0),\n\nwhere each δ i is 0 or 1 and P k\n\ni=0 δ i=r.\n\nIn particular (see the assertion after the proof of Theorem 4 in [6]), for a\n\ntriangular number N we obtain the following result quoted by Gardner in 1983\n\n[11]–Brandt’s Equilibrium Theorem.\n\nCorollary (Brandt’s Equilibrium Theorem) If N= 1 + 2 + · · · +k, then (k, k −\n\n1,...,1) is the unique T-cyclic partition of N.\n\nRecall that the above theorem follows from Theorem 4 of Akin and Davis\n\n[1] whose proof is based on Brandt’s result. Theorem 5 in [1] which is proved\n\ndirectly, also gives a description of all T-cyclic partitions for arbitrary N as in\n\nabove theorem. The above corollary is proved by Etienne [10] by introducing a\n\nnatural array representation of a partition λ. The idea in his proof is applied in\n\nthe proof of Theorem 2.1 in [12] (the above theorem) to general N.\n\nIn the second version of this paper, we will give a new induction proof of the\n\nabove theorem [27].\n\n5\n\n3. Some variations and extensions of the game Bulgarian\n\nSolitaire\n\nMany variants of BS were invented after 1980, such as in 1985 Austrian Soli-\n\ntaire by E. Akin and M. Davis (USA) [1], in 1992 Montreal Solitaire by C. Can-\n\nnings and J. Haigh (England) [7], in 1995 (without name) by R. Servedio and\n\nY.N. Yeh [31], in 1995 (without name) by Y.N. Yeh, in 1997 Carolina Solitaire\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA007075 - OEIS (https://oeis.org/A007075/internal)\nciteturn9721search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Crawled: 4 months ago; Cannings, J.Haigh, <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/0097-3165(92)90037-U\">Montreal solitaire</a>, J.\n\n(Greetings from The On-Line Encyclopedia of Integer Sequences!)\n\nA007075\n\nNumber of irreducible positions of size n in Montreal solitaire.\n(Formerly M1441)\n\n6\n\n`%I M1441 #33 Dec 20 2017 04:12:10 `\n\n`%S 1,2,5,13,35,95,260,714,1965,5415,14934,41206,113730,313958,866801, `\n\n`%T 2393315,6608473,18248017,50389350,139144906,384237186,1061044865, `\n\n`%U 2930013158,8091077148,22343115337,61699480866,170380367189,470497972866 `\n\n`%N Number of irreducible positions of size n in Montreal solitaire. `\n\n`%C a(n) is also the number of indecomposable permutations with exactly n inversions; there is one indecomposable permutation with no inversions. - _David Bevan_, Dec 19 2017 `\n\n`%D N. J. A. Sloane and Simon Plouffe, The Encyclopedia of Integer Sequences, Academic Press, 1995 (includes this sequence). `\n\n`%H David Bevan, <a href=\"/A007075/b007075.txt\">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..1000</a> `\n\n`%H C. Cannings, J. Haigh, <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/0097-3165(92)90037-U\">Montreal solitaire</a>, J. Combin. Theory Ser. A 60 (1992), no. 1, 50-66. `\n\n`%F a(n) = d(n, 1) where d(n, k) is defined in A007046. - _Sean A. Irvine_, Oct 06 2017 `\n\n`%F The ordinary generating function is f(1), where f(v) satisfies the functional equation f(v) = v*(1 + f(1 + x*v) - f(1)). The variable x marks inversions and v marks left-to-right minima. - _David Bevan_, Dec 19 2017 `\n\n`%e a(3) = 5; five indecomposable permutations have three inversions: 321, 2341, 2413, 3142, 4123. - _David Bevan_, Dec 19 2017 `\n\n`%t r[1,1]=1;r[_,0]:=0;r[n_,k_]:=r[n,k]=Sum[r[n-k,j]Binomial[j+1,k],{j,k-1,(Sqrt[8(n-k)+1]-1)/2}];a[n_]:=Sum[r[n,k],{k,(Sqrt[8n+1]-1)/2}];Array[a,20] (* _David Bevan_, Dec 19 2017 *) `\n\n`%Y Cf. A007046, A007048, A007049, A007050, A007076. `\n\n`%K nonn `\n\n`%O 1,2 `\n\n`%A _N. J. A. Sloane_, _Mira Bernstein_ `\n\n`%E More terms from _Sean A. Irvine_, Oct 06 2017 `\n\n``--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nChri